00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:13.920
And today, we'll talk about how
Azure Managed Redis is the new

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:17.880
age Redis, Managed Redis
on Azure, and how it helps

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:22.110
with building AI apps
at the internet scale.

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:25.890
So most of you must be
familiar with Redis.

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:27.720
It has been around
for a long time.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:31.320
You are probably using it
already for your session store,

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:34.650
for leaderboard, for
reducing database cost,

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:38.640
or making your databases
more performant.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:42.420
Redis use cases have
recently expanded along

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:43.680
with the internet scale.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:46.800
It started out with some
basic traditional scenarios,

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:51.750
but Redis now has many more
use cases that it can support,

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:54.150
like time series,
vector searches,

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:57.210
JSON data, semantic caching.

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:58.620
Times have changed.

00:00:58.620 --> 00:00:59.820
The internet scale has changed,

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:04.050
and so has the Redis use
cases have expanded as well.

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:07.590
So today, we'll be quickly
covering what's new with Redis,

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:10.650
especially the Azure
Managed Redis.

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:15.860
Azure Managed Redis is a fully
managed in-memory data store

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:16.490
on Azure.

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:20.840
It's a first-party offering
on Azure, and it's based

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:23.090
on the Redis
Enterprise software,

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:27.290
which offers a much more
cost-effective TCO compared

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:29.390
to the Azure Cache
for Redis offering.

00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:31.340
And it's also much
more performant.

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:35.700
It's up to 15% more -- 15
times, sorry, not 15% --

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:38.250
15 times much more performant

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:40.290
than the Azure Cache
for Redis offerings.

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:42.060
It is zone redundant by default.

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:45.330
It has four nines offering
by default, and obviously,

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:49.320
you can also set it up
with a five 9s setup

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:50.640
with a geo-replication,

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:54.990
which will give you the five
9s of availability SLA.

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:57.150
As with the Redis software,

00:01:57.150 --> 00:02:01.860
it can support your
accelerations scenarios

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:03.600
for your applications.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:05.940
You can use Redis as
a distributed cache.

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:07.680
You can use it for
session management.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:09.930
You can use it for
leaderboards, pub/sub.

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:10.530
You name it.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:13.290
There's so many scenarios
where you can use Redis.

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:17.670
But alongside those, Azure
Managed Redis is also able

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:21.090
to power your RAG scenarios,
and you can also use it

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:25.800
as a lightweight vector
data store as well.

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:27.240
How is that possible?

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:30.750
So we'll talk about the
secret source a little bit.

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:33.240
How does Azure Managed
Redis do that?

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:37.470
But first, let's look at the
state of AI agents today.

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:41.460
So AI agents today -- this
is a very simple depiction,

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:45.660
but there's an input that
usually a user provides,

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:48.240
or there's some
triggers or events

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:49.980
that trigger the AI agent.

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:53.250
And internally, the AI agent
is essentially a bunch

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:56.700
of instructions, a model,
and a lot of tool calls

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:59.250
that enrich what
the agent can do.

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:01.590
And then obviously, it
produces some kind of output

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:05.010
that makes it more --
that makes it productive.

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:08.160
But if you look at the tool
calls that the agent needs

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:12.300
to enrich itself, it needs
knowledge that's specific

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:13.290
to your domain.

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:15.750
It needs to be able to
execute some actions

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:18.630
so that it's more impactful,
and then obviously,

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:21.000
it needs memory as well.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:24.510
So these are -- this is
a high-level overview

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:27.090
of what actually an
agent looks like.

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:32.550
But if you start doing
agents in production,

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:35.640
lot of challenges
start coming up.

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:37.980
Gartner predicts that 40%

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:42.480
of enterprise applications
will have specific AI agents

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:44.250
by the year 2026.

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:50.160
So this is already up from 2025,
where 2025 didn't have a lot

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:53.820
of AI-specific --
task-specific agents,

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:57.330
and that number is
growing up this year,

00:03:57.330 --> 00:03:59.940
and it will continue
next year as well.

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:03.910
But once you start putting
AI agents in production,

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:07.350
there's some challenges
that are going to show up.

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:11.640
The pace of innovation in
the AI world is tremendous.

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:13.920
How do you keep
up with the pace?

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:17.190
How do you keep yourself abreast
with the new innovations,

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:19.080
the new things that are
happening in vector search?

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:23.070
How do you use or how do
you adopt those things

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:24.690
for your production scenarios?

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:25.770
So that's one challenge.

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:27.990
The other one is
unpredictable cost.

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:33.740
A lot of this is new for most
of us, the way tokens work,

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:37.130
the way token score relate
to the dollar amount

00:04:37.130 --> 00:04:38.810
that you end up paying.

00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:42.530
A lot of times, you will see --
you get surprises at the end

00:04:42.530 --> 00:04:44.300
of the month when
you see your bill.

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:46.500
So there's unpredictable
cost in there.

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:51.780
Model calls are expensive, and
they take a lot of time as well.

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:55.950
So there's this cost aspect as
well that's quite challenging.

00:04:55.950 --> 00:05:00.000
And these agents, as we just
saw in the previous screen,

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.780
agents need context,
and agents need memory

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:06.120
to be more impactful.

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:09.060
Once you solve all
of these challenges,

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:11.880
that's when you will be able
to build enterprise-ready,

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:15.510
internet-scale AI agents.

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:18.900
So today, we'll be mostly
covering these two challenges:

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:21.540
the cost management and
context and memory.

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:24.240
How do you solve
these two challenges

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:25.920
with Azure Managed Redis?

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:28.110
So I have my friends
Phil and Roy,

00:05:28.110 --> 00:05:31.170
who will join a little
bit later, who will talk

00:05:31.170 --> 00:05:33.750
about these challenges
one by one.

00:05:33.750 --> 00:05:37.050
So how does Azure
Managed Redis do that?

00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:42.210
So the secret sauce is the Redis
search module that comes in,

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:45.090
that you can configure
at no extra cost.

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:49.590
So the Redis search module
allows Azure Managed Redis

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:53.670
to do vector similarity
search on embeddings

00:05:53.670 --> 00:05:55.920
that you store inside of Redis.

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:59.490
So once you configure this
module, it allows you

00:05:59.490 --> 00:06:03.000
to do a similarity
search on vectors,

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:08.190
and that powers both the cost
savings and the context --

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:11.970
the memory context scenarios
that I just mentioned.

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:15.030
Now that I've given up the
secret sauce, I think it's time

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:19.260
for me to take a backseat
and let Phil and Roy later

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:22.410
on show you all the magic
and how it happens.

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:24.870
So this was it from my side.

00:06:24.870 --> 00:06:27.330
Take it over, Phil.

00:06:27.330 --> 00:06:27.900
PHILIP LAUSSERMAIR:
Hi, everyone.

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:29.370
My name is Philip Laussermair.

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:32.640
I'm a Senior Solutions Architect
at Redis, and I specialize

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:34.260
in Azure Managed Redis.

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:37.020
As Shruti mentioned, I'm really
excited to walk you through one

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:40.500
of our top AI use cases,
semantic caching,

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:41.490
and it's all powered

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:45.480
by our semantic search
capability in Redis Search.

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:50.070
So if you know Redis, you
probably think of caching.

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:52.470
This is one of our
bread-and-butter use cases.

00:06:52.470 --> 00:06:54.930
This is what we actually
were founded on.

00:06:54.930 --> 00:06:57.270
And for the last 10 years or so,

00:06:57.270 --> 00:06:59.190
this is really what
we focused on.

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:02.190
And the typical pattern,
you see it here.

00:07:02.190 --> 00:07:03.720
You put it in front
of a database.

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:06.840
It's meant to offload a
lot of repetitive costs,

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:10.740
take off pressure from the
database, improve performance.

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:12.660
And that's the typical pattern.

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:13.740
You check the cache,

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:16.200
if the existing key
is already in there.

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:18.450
If it is, you serve
it to the customer.

00:07:18.450 --> 00:07:20.940
If not, you call the database
and then load it back

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:22.800
into your Redis instance.

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:25.740
So this works extremely well.

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:28.170
In any best practice
with databases,

00:07:28.170 --> 00:07:31.290
you'll probably see caching come
up as one of the first ways

00:07:31.290 --> 00:07:33.240
to optimize and improve.

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:35.460
But a lot of things, as
Shruti has mentioned,

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:39.930
has changed in the last decade
or so, especially in the last,

00:07:39.930 --> 00:07:41.550
let's say, two or three years

00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:44.850
with the advent
of generative AI.

00:07:44.850 --> 00:07:46.980
So, at Redis, what
we've been able

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:54.180
to do is exact key-value match
caching is not ideal for LLMs.

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:57.210
I could ask a query
that Shruti could send

00:07:57.210 --> 00:08:00.510
in a few minutes later in a
slightly different variation,

00:08:00.510 --> 00:08:02.550
but it could be the
same underlying intent.

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:04.110
It could have the same meaning.

00:08:04.110 --> 00:08:07.140
And in that scenario,
tokens are expensive.

00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:10.140
You're going to generate a full
amount of tokens for a scenario

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:12.930
where you potentially
could have saved on that.

00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:16.350
And that's where semantic
caching comes into play.

00:08:16.350 --> 00:08:19.590
So the general idea
stays the same.

00:08:19.590 --> 00:08:23.940
We're going to check Redis,
see if there's a cache hit.

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:25.740
If not, instead of
calling a database,

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:27.660
we're going to call an LLM.

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:31.620
But really how this is all being
powered is now you can store

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:34.830
your vector embeddings
in your Redis instance,

00:08:34.830 --> 00:08:38.970
and then using vector similarity
search, as prompts come in,

00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:42.450
we can then check the cache,
compare the similarity,

00:08:42.450 --> 00:08:46.080
and based on a threshold that
you would set, that determines

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:49.730
if it was similar enough
to be a cache hit or not.

00:08:49.730 --> 00:08:51.080
And we actually have a demo,

00:08:51.080 --> 00:08:53.780
so I can show you this live
what this would look like,

00:08:53.780 --> 00:08:56.390
how it would be for
you or your customers.

00:08:56.390 --> 00:08:59.120
But at the end of the day, the
idea is this is really meant

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:02.100
to save on tokens,
which we all see

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:04.320
in the news day in and day out.

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:07.020
Token cost is exploding.

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:11.130
Response time, of course, is
a huge performance factor.

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:14.160
The agents are running
longer and longer.

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:17.190
They're getting much more
intelligent, but that could lead

00:09:17.190 --> 00:09:20.450
to seconds or even
minutes of wait time.

00:09:20.450 --> 00:09:23.180
And then finally, consistency.

00:09:23.180 --> 00:09:26.750
There's certain use cases where
you sometimes don't want an LLM

00:09:26.750 --> 00:09:28.580
to be so non-deterministic.

00:09:28.580 --> 00:09:30.870
If you're going to ask
it the same prompt

00:09:30.870 --> 00:09:34.380
or the same underlying intent
every time, you want to be able

00:09:34.380 --> 00:09:36.180
to have a sense of confidence

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:41.010
that you're promoting the
same answer that you intend.

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:44.220
So it gives you some confidence
from that angle as well.

00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:46.770
But now that we have a
general understanding

00:09:46.770 --> 00:09:50.760
of the underlying architecture,
why don't we jump into a demo,

00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:54.390
and that should make it a lot
more clear and pretty exciting.

00:09:54.390 --> 00:09:58.050
Okay. So now we're in our
semantic caching demo here.

00:09:58.050 --> 00:09:59.850
So just to orient you, I want

00:09:59.850 --> 00:10:02.250
to show you what you're
looking at here.

00:10:02.250 --> 00:10:05.880
On the left side, I want you to
think of just a regular LLM,

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:09.110
take Copilot, ChatGPT, Claude.

00:10:09.110 --> 00:10:10.310
That's the left side.

00:10:10.310 --> 00:10:12.050
The right side is the same idea,

00:10:12.050 --> 00:10:14.930
but with semantic caching
underneath the hood,

00:10:14.930 --> 00:10:17.600
powered by an Azure
Managed Redis instance,

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:19.850
live as we go along.

00:10:19.850 --> 00:10:22.640
I want to point out a few other
items, some metrics down here

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:25.830
that will capture every
time we send a prompt.

00:10:25.830 --> 00:10:28.620
But really, without further
ado, we can jump right in.

00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:31.860
So let's say we were
going to send a prompt,

00:10:31.860 --> 00:10:34.230
"How do I improve product
search relevance using

00:10:34.230 --> 00:10:35.370
vector embeddings?"

00:10:35.370 --> 00:10:39.300
Let's pretend we're an
e-commerce company, Contoso,

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:43.010
and this might be a typical
question that would be asked.

00:10:43.010 --> 00:10:45.950
So what's happening right
now is both are acting

00:10:45.950 --> 00:10:47.690
like a typical LLM.

00:10:47.690 --> 00:10:48.680
I sent a query.

00:10:48.680 --> 00:10:50.060
We're going to get a response.

00:10:50.060 --> 00:10:52.520
They were two
separate responses.

00:10:52.520 --> 00:10:56.190
So generative AI being
non-deterministic.

00:10:56.190 --> 00:10:58.170
You can see the same query

00:10:58.170 --> 00:11:02.220
at relatively the same exact
moment gave me similar

00:11:02.220 --> 00:11:04.320
but a little different answers.

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:06.480
The one on the left
took 118 tokens.

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:08.580
The one on the right, 131.

00:11:08.580 --> 00:11:13.470
And both were roughly between
one to two seconds in timing.

00:11:13.470 --> 00:11:18.180
So now let's take a
similarly phrased search,

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:20.490
but not exactly the same value.

00:11:20.490 --> 00:11:23.970
So instead of "How do I improve
product search relevance using

00:11:23.970 --> 00:11:25.380
vector embeddings?"

00:11:25.380 --> 00:11:26.310
let's just change it up,

00:11:26.310 --> 00:11:29.520
"How can vector embeddings make
product search more relevant?"

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:33.660
And what I want you to take a
focus on is right on the right,

00:11:33.660 --> 00:11:36.690
what you saw was a
semantic cache hit.

00:11:36.690 --> 00:11:39.990
So the first thing you notice
was it was extremely fast.

00:11:39.990 --> 00:11:42.870
The second thing you'll
notice is it is the exact

00:11:42.870 --> 00:11:44.460
same response.

00:11:44.460 --> 00:11:47.670
So we're taking advantage
of that previous response.

00:11:47.670 --> 00:11:50.040
And so no tokens were generated.

00:11:50.040 --> 00:11:52.140
That's one of the biggest
things to highlight.

00:11:52.140 --> 00:11:55.890
On the left here, since this
was just our normal LLM call,

00:11:55.890 --> 00:11:58.620
every time we send a request,
we're going to get a response.

00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:02.430
It did go and generate
another 126 tokens.

00:12:02.430 --> 00:12:07.110
As you can see, very similar,
but for this kind of query,

00:12:07.110 --> 00:12:08.940
we didn't get to take
advantage of the previous one.

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:12.840
So every time, it's just
going to make net new calls.

00:12:12.840 --> 00:12:15.030
So I want to show
you one other thing.

00:12:15.030 --> 00:12:18.090
If I click here on this
query, these are the steps

00:12:18.090 --> 00:12:20.640
that actually happened
underneath the hood

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:25.200
in that almost millisecond it
took to get the response back.

00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:27.600
So the first few things,
first off, of course,

00:12:27.600 --> 00:12:30.450
we're going to check the cache.

00:12:30.450 --> 00:12:32.820
There's different ways you can
optimize semantic caching.

00:12:32.820 --> 00:12:37.080
The first one being we actually
can do an exact cache lookup.

00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:40.710
So if it was a key-value pair,
we could take advantage of that

00:12:40.710 --> 00:12:44.520
and actually just serve it
like a data cache would.

00:12:44.520 --> 00:12:46.530
Since that didn't
exist, we then went

00:12:46.530 --> 00:12:48.360
and generated our embeddings.

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:50.100
We got our embeddings back,

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:53.580
and did the vector
similarity search.

00:12:53.580 --> 00:12:54.630
Based on our threshold,

00:12:54.630 --> 00:12:58.020
the cosine similarity
was high enough

00:12:58.020 --> 00:13:00.150
that it triggered a cache hit.

00:13:00.150 --> 00:13:03.270
And so that's what you
saw as the end user.

00:13:03.270 --> 00:13:08.910
You got that result super fast,
with no tokens being spent.

00:13:08.910 --> 00:13:11.010
So just to show you what
it would look like again

00:13:11.010 --> 00:13:11.850
in the same scenario

00:13:11.850 --> 00:13:14.940
with another query not
related to any of these.

00:13:14.940 --> 00:13:17.520
If I fire it off, both
are going to act just

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:20.220
like typical LLMs, respond.

00:13:20.220 --> 00:13:23.580
The reason I highlight that is
this is all running every single

00:13:23.580 --> 00:13:25.290
time underneath the hood.

00:13:25.290 --> 00:13:29.370
Because Redis is an in-memory
database, it's lightning fast.

00:13:29.370 --> 00:13:32.070
So just because
this is happening,

00:13:32.070 --> 00:13:34.590
customers aren't
sitting around waiting.

00:13:34.590 --> 00:13:37.860
If I throw in more,
you'll see it's just --

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:41.340
you wouldn't notice it was
running any other time.

00:13:41.340 --> 00:13:45.510
So next, I want to show
some more deep dives

00:13:45.510 --> 00:13:47.310
on vector similarity search.

00:13:47.310 --> 00:13:50.640
So taking our first
prompt, let's see,

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:52.800
"What's the most cost-effective
caching strategy

00:13:52.800 --> 00:13:55.590
for serving personalized
product recommendations

00:13:55.590 --> 00:13:58.560
to 10 million daily
active users?"

00:13:58.560 --> 00:14:00.660
Then we have a few
variations below

00:14:00.660 --> 00:14:04.380
that are either extremely
similar or even unrelated.

00:14:04.380 --> 00:14:07.350
So what I'm going to do is
I'm just going to run a test

00:14:07.350 --> 00:14:11.790
that is generating the
similarity using a cosine vector

00:14:11.790 --> 00:14:13.380
similarity search.

00:14:13.380 --> 00:14:16.950
And this similarity threshold
here is what you would set

00:14:16.950 --> 00:14:18.960
in your application or client.

00:14:18.960 --> 00:14:21.630
That's the threshold
that you are comfortable

00:14:21.630 --> 00:14:26.280
with for what would trigger
a semantic cache hit or not.

00:14:26.280 --> 00:14:30.960
So just here what we generated,
at a 50% similarity threshold,

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:32.340
it would need to clear.

00:14:32.340 --> 00:14:35.880
We can see that these top four
prompts would be considered a

00:14:35.880 --> 00:14:37.950
semantic cache hit.

00:14:37.950 --> 00:14:41.760
If we wanted to increase that,
let's say we wanted to go

00:14:41.760 --> 00:14:46.410
to 75%, the first thing you
notice is this variation four.

00:14:46.410 --> 00:14:48.420
It's no longer considered
similar enough

00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:51.330
to do a semantic cache hit.

00:14:51.330 --> 00:14:54.120
I should call out this first
one, prompt cache hit.

00:14:54.120 --> 00:14:56.460
This is that exact
same key-value pair.

00:14:56.460 --> 00:15:00.210
So this is going to be a
match 100% of the time

00:15:00.210 --> 00:15:02.850
because it's an exact
key-value pair.

00:15:02.850 --> 00:15:05.250
And finally, if we wanted
to be super strict

00:15:05.250 --> 00:15:08.070
and went all the way up
to 90%, every single one

00:15:08.070 --> 00:15:10.110
of these would be a miss.

00:15:10.110 --> 00:15:11.520
So that gives you
the flexibility

00:15:11.520 --> 00:15:15.000
of how strict you want
each scenario to be

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:18.760
with your threshold using
vector similarity search.

00:15:19.800 --> 00:15:21.870
Finally, I want to show
you another scenario

00:15:21.870 --> 00:15:27.270
of what it looks like for your
end users that are being powered

00:15:27.270 --> 00:15:29.820
by the same Managed
Redis instance,

00:15:29.820 --> 00:15:32.580
where you might be thinking,
"Okay, this is great.

00:15:32.580 --> 00:15:34.260
I love these
semantic cache hits.

00:15:34.260 --> 00:15:35.520
It saves a lot of tokens,

00:15:35.520 --> 00:15:37.890
but how do I ensure
no data leakage?"

00:15:37.890 --> 00:15:40.320
If someone asks a very
similar question,

00:15:40.320 --> 00:15:44.820
but they have specific data to
them that the LLM is using,

00:15:44.820 --> 00:15:47.430
how do I make sure that's not
being shown to anyone else?

00:15:47.430 --> 00:15:51.030
So we have our two users
here: Merchandiser Mia

00:15:51.030 --> 00:15:55.350
and Platform Priya, and they
have different roles in Contoso.

00:15:55.350 --> 00:15:57.870
And what we're going to do
is we're just going to ask,

00:15:57.870 --> 00:16:02.370
based on some memory we have on
the users, "What is your role?"

00:16:02.370 --> 00:16:04.710
So on the left,
Merchandiser Mia,

00:16:04.710 --> 00:16:06.900
on the right, Platform Priya.

00:16:06.900 --> 00:16:12.810
And I'm going to show you again,
first, LLM call, nothing there.

00:16:12.810 --> 00:16:14.760
Let's say, "What's my job?"

00:16:14.760 --> 00:16:16.260
If I send that one,
what you're going

00:16:16.260 --> 00:16:19.560
to notice is each got a semantic
cache hit, but strictly

00:16:19.560 --> 00:16:22.380
on the keys we have
saved for them.

00:16:22.380 --> 00:16:25.770
And how that's being done is in
the way you structure your keys

00:16:25.770 --> 00:16:27.870
in Redis, you would actually set

00:16:27.870 --> 00:16:31.350
up the naming convention
specific to each user or domain

00:16:31.350 --> 00:16:33.720
or whatever fits your use case,

00:16:33.720 --> 00:16:37.050
and the search engine
can be queried based

00:16:37.050 --> 00:16:39.480
on that formula, essentially.

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:42.720
So in this example, we want
to make sure that it's

00:16:42.720 --> 00:16:47.760
in a per-user format so that
there's no chance of leakage.

00:16:47.760 --> 00:16:49.500
On the flip side,
there are cases

00:16:49.500 --> 00:16:52.890
where you actually
want a global cache,

00:16:52.890 --> 00:16:56.010
and that one would be
something like an FAQ bot,

00:16:56.010 --> 00:16:58.110
where it doesn't matter
who the user is.

00:16:58.110 --> 00:17:00.060
They're all going to be
asking similar questions,

00:17:00.060 --> 00:17:02.700
and you really want to
take advantage of those.

00:17:02.700 --> 00:17:06.510
So let's pretend this is our
FAQ bot on our Contoso website

00:17:06.510 --> 00:17:09.480
that unauthenticated
users can come and use.

00:17:09.480 --> 00:17:11.130
So let's start with
the first one:

00:17:11.130 --> 00:17:13.410
"What are just --
what are your hours?"

00:17:13.410 --> 00:17:15.720
And these are
semantically different.

00:17:15.720 --> 00:17:17.340
This one was
considered a cache hit

00:17:17.340 --> 00:17:19.410
because it was similar
enough to this.

00:17:19.410 --> 00:17:22.320
This is just a vanilla LLM,
so it doesn't have hours.

00:17:22.320 --> 00:17:26.160
But you can start to see where,
in these public instances,

00:17:26.160 --> 00:17:29.340
you can generate a lot
of semantic cache hits

00:17:29.340 --> 00:17:30.990
and save a lot of tokens.

00:17:30.990 --> 00:17:34.260
If this visitor B was a user
that was coming in, say,

00:17:34.260 --> 00:17:35.880
five minutes later,

00:17:35.880 --> 00:17:38.400
they wouldn't know any different
based on these responses,

00:17:38.400 --> 00:17:40.710
obviously using
real company data.

00:17:40.710 --> 00:17:43.200
But in that scenario,
almost every response

00:17:43.200 --> 00:17:45.690
in this chain would be
a semantic cache hit,

00:17:45.690 --> 00:17:49.350
and there would be no tokens
spent in that scenario.

00:17:49.350 --> 00:17:52.020
So, again, one of the
benefits and the flexibility

00:17:52.020 --> 00:17:55.590
of either a global
or per-user cache.

00:17:55.590 --> 00:17:57.660
Finally, just to wrap up,
I want to give you a sense

00:17:57.660 --> 00:18:00.060
of what does this
look like at scale.

00:18:00.060 --> 00:18:02.760
So a quick calculator
of what this looks

00:18:02.760 --> 00:18:05.040
like in an enterprise use case.

00:18:05.040 --> 00:18:09.210
Let's even drop this down to
say 200,000 daily queries,

00:18:09.210 --> 00:18:14.940
a 70% semantic cache hit rate,
extremely low on the token sort

00:18:14.940 --> 00:18:19.200
of budget in this scenario,
and the pricing of GPT-5.

00:18:19.200 --> 00:18:22.500
So, in this scenario,
on an annualized basis,

00:18:22.500 --> 00:18:23.460
that would be just

00:18:23.460 --> 00:18:28.440
about $300,000 a year
for your token spend.

00:18:28.440 --> 00:18:30.690
And the way to think
about the savings

00:18:30.690 --> 00:18:34.260
with semantic caching is
whatever your cache hit rate

00:18:34.260 --> 00:18:37.110
percentage is, that
is your net savings.

00:18:37.110 --> 00:18:39.240
So with a 70% cache hit rate,

00:18:39.240 --> 00:18:43.530
we're going to get 70% savings
off of this overall spend.

00:18:43.530 --> 00:18:45.870
And so what that
comes out to is just

00:18:45.870 --> 00:18:49.050
under 100,000, around
90,000 a year.

00:18:49.050 --> 00:18:51.780
This also factors in
both the embedding cost

00:18:51.780 --> 00:18:54.420
that you would have to
pay for for each query,

00:18:54.420 --> 00:18:55.920
and the actual Redis instance

00:18:55.920 --> 00:18:58.680
that could power
a load like this.

00:18:58.680 --> 00:19:00.570
And as you can see,
even factoring those

00:19:00.570 --> 00:19:06.450
in over an annualized basis,
that would save over $200,000.

00:19:06.450 --> 00:19:08.700
The last thing before I
wrap up is I want to call

00:19:08.700 --> 00:19:10.830
out this break-even rate.

00:19:10.830 --> 00:19:14.670
This is the number of semantic
cache hits that you would need

00:19:14.670 --> 00:19:18.630
to have in your setup for
this to be break-even.

00:19:18.630 --> 00:19:22.380
So what this is saying,
at a 1.2% cache hit rate,

00:19:22.380 --> 00:19:25.160
you only need one out of
100 queries in your setup

00:19:25.160 --> 00:19:27.440
to be a semantic
cache hit for this

00:19:27.440 --> 00:19:32.330
to ultimately save you any money
net after that first query.

00:19:32.330 --> 00:19:35.700
So, again, it's very scalable.

00:19:36.870 --> 00:19:39.840
Once you set in place, you
can kind of leave them there.

00:19:39.840 --> 00:19:41.310
It really helps
with performance.

00:19:41.310 --> 00:19:42.750
It helps with token spend.

00:19:42.750 --> 00:19:45.210
But overall, that
is semantic caching

00:19:45.210 --> 00:19:46.740
with Azure Managed Redis.

00:19:46.740 --> 00:19:49.620
And now I am happy to
pass it over to Roy,

00:19:49.620 --> 00:19:50.550
who's going to talk a little bit

00:19:50.550 --> 00:19:53.190
about how you can use
Azure Managed Redis

00:19:53.190 --> 00:19:55.760
for agent memory
at the same time.

00:19:55.760 --> 00:19:57.300
Thanks, everyone.

00:19:57.300 --> 00:19:58.080
ROY DE MILDE: Thank you, Phil.

00:19:58.080 --> 00:20:00.660
And also, Shruti, thank you
for the great presentation

00:20:00.660 --> 00:20:03.000
and the demo and
everything that we saw.

00:20:03.000 --> 00:20:04.650
So I'm Roy, based out
of the Netherlands.

00:20:04.650 --> 00:20:07.500
I'm a Solutions Architect
for Azure Managed Redis,

00:20:07.500 --> 00:20:10.530
covering EMEA, and working
with customers and partners

00:20:10.530 --> 00:20:11.640
to make them successful

00:20:11.640 --> 00:20:14.790
when they are adopting
Azure Managed Redis.

00:20:14.790 --> 00:20:17.220
I'm going to talk a little
bit about agent memory.

00:20:17.220 --> 00:20:20.250
I'm just going to explain
why that is important

00:20:20.250 --> 00:20:23.220
and how you can use AMR for
that, and then I will jump

00:20:23.220 --> 00:20:26.340
into a quick demo as
well to showcase that.

00:20:26.340 --> 00:20:29.040
So when we think about
an agentic workflow

00:20:29.040 --> 00:20:31.500
or an agentic AI
system, we're thinking

00:20:31.500 --> 00:20:37.080
about different agents
working together to make sure

00:20:37.080 --> 00:20:39.750
that you have highly
fulfilling AI tasks.

00:20:39.750 --> 00:20:43.350
But those agents can act even
better when you're thinking

00:20:43.350 --> 00:20:47.370
about when they can
leverage memory as well.

00:20:47.370 --> 00:20:48.780
The way we determine this is

00:20:48.780 --> 00:20:52.650
that we have these short-term
memory and the long-term memory.

00:20:52.650 --> 00:20:55.350
So, for short-term
memory, we often think

00:20:55.350 --> 00:20:59.880
about the interaction, the
conversation, a certain state,

00:20:59.880 --> 00:21:03.240
and stuff like that,
that we are saving

00:21:03.240 --> 00:21:06.480
within that short-term
memory capability.

00:21:06.480 --> 00:21:08.730
But then, if you look at
the long-term memory,

00:21:08.730 --> 00:21:11.490
we think about from a
personalization perspective.

00:21:11.490 --> 00:21:15.510
When a user is interacting
with the AI system,

00:21:15.510 --> 00:21:18.360
you basically want the
AI system to be aware.

00:21:18.360 --> 00:21:20.100
What are the preferences?

00:21:20.100 --> 00:21:22.290
What is some of the
things that are important

00:21:22.290 --> 00:21:24.000
for this specific user?

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:27.390
Or what is something of their
interest and stuff like that?

00:21:27.390 --> 00:21:29.190
And you can go nuts with this.

00:21:29.190 --> 00:21:31.860
The world's your oyster
if you think about it.

00:21:31.860 --> 00:21:34.440
So what we are doing is
splitting those two out,

00:21:34.440 --> 00:21:38.760
more on the conversation
history and state aspect,

00:21:38.760 --> 00:21:44.220
and also the preferences, the
behavior, the durable facts,

00:21:44.220 --> 00:21:46.860
and the interest for a
real personalization type

00:21:46.860 --> 00:21:49.680
of interaction.

00:21:49.680 --> 00:21:52.110
Now, when you think about this,
and a user is interacting

00:21:52.110 --> 00:21:57.540
with your systems, now the
agents can have rapidly fast

00:21:57.540 --> 00:21:59.220
access to that data.

00:21:59.220 --> 00:22:02.010
And they can use that when they
are determining they're using

00:22:02.010 --> 00:22:05.340
specific tools, they're
creating, they're using an LLM

00:22:05.340 --> 00:22:09.180
to do certain reasoning about
it, whatever you have built,

00:22:09.180 --> 00:22:12.900
but they can rapidly fast
get that data and include

00:22:12.900 --> 00:22:16.350
that within the whole
pattern that they are doing.

00:22:16.350 --> 00:22:19.320
And this is where Azure Managed
Redis really, really shines

00:22:19.320 --> 00:22:24.420
if you are saving that
specific data into AMR.

00:22:24.420 --> 00:22:28.050
And also think about when
agents are working together,

00:22:28.050 --> 00:22:33.780
and a specific agent wants to
give context to the other agent,

00:22:33.780 --> 00:22:36.780
it's stored within AMR, so
it can just easily say,

00:22:36.780 --> 00:22:38.460
"Hey, have a look at this."

00:22:38.460 --> 00:22:40.830
And now the new agent that
needs to do something else

00:22:40.830 --> 00:22:44.370
within the agentic workflow,
they can grab that information

00:22:44.370 --> 00:22:48.690
as well, giving you a way
better experience as a user,

00:22:48.690 --> 00:22:51.570
but also allowing you
to interact better

00:22:51.570 --> 00:22:54.390
with your consumers
and your users.

00:22:54.390 --> 00:22:56.400
So I do have a small
demo around this.

00:22:56.400 --> 00:22:58.890
So I'm going to hop
over to another screen.

00:22:58.890 --> 00:23:01.570
Just give me one sec real quick.

00:23:09.270 --> 00:23:11.700
So here you can see the
demo that we have built.

00:23:11.700 --> 00:23:14.430
So basically, it's the same
context of the previous demo

00:23:14.430 --> 00:23:17.340
that you have seen by Phil.

00:23:17.340 --> 00:23:18.510
So on the left-hand side,

00:23:18.510 --> 00:23:20.400
you see the without
memory capabilities,

00:23:20.400 --> 00:23:21.450
and on the right-hand side,

00:23:21.450 --> 00:23:23.490
you see the with
memory capabilities.

00:23:23.490 --> 00:23:25.740
So this is everything
around personalization,

00:23:25.740 --> 00:23:27.510
and that's how we
have built this.

00:23:27.510 --> 00:23:30.690
So this is basically an AI
system where I can interact

00:23:30.690 --> 00:23:33.510
with the system, and the
system knows about me.

00:23:33.510 --> 00:23:36.120
It has stored the
conversations that we had,

00:23:36.120 --> 00:23:39.300
and it has stored the durable
facts, the preferences,

00:23:39.300 --> 00:23:41.940
what type of work do I do.

00:23:41.940 --> 00:23:45.300
So in this scenario, let
me hop over to myself,

00:23:45.300 --> 00:23:49.500
and let me just basically teach
the AI a little bit about me.

00:23:49.500 --> 00:23:51.960
So I'm going to say, "Hey, I'm
a software engineer working

00:23:51.960 --> 00:23:54.270
on machine learning
projects, and I love hiking,

00:23:54.270 --> 00:23:56.220
photography on the weekends."

00:23:56.220 --> 00:23:58.470
So when I'm going to send that
to both, you're going to see

00:23:58.470 --> 00:24:00.540
on the left-hand side, it's
going to respond like,

00:24:00.540 --> 00:24:01.470
"Hey, nice to meet you.

00:24:01.470 --> 00:24:03.780
Great." But nothing
is being saved --

00:24:03.780 --> 00:24:05.520
not on the conversational side,

00:24:05.520 --> 00:24:07.680
not on the long-term
memory side.

00:24:07.680 --> 00:24:09.600
On the right-hand
side, you actually see

00:24:09.600 --> 00:24:11.790
that we are analyzing
the message.

00:24:11.790 --> 00:24:14.070
We can give a response
back, and we're going

00:24:14.070 --> 00:24:16.920
to remember certain
facts about this user.

00:24:16.920 --> 00:24:18.390
In this case, this is me.

00:24:18.390 --> 00:24:21.390
So the user is a software
engineer, and it loves hiking,

00:24:21.390 --> 00:24:22.860
and it loves photography.

00:24:22.860 --> 00:24:25.740
And if we actually look to
the agent activity log,

00:24:25.740 --> 00:24:29.880
it runs through a specific
aspect of things to do

00:24:29.880 --> 00:24:33.660
to analyze the message,
reason about the message,

00:24:33.660 --> 00:24:37.380
and also saving the aspects
that we want to save

00:24:37.380 --> 00:24:39.270
to the long-term memory.

00:24:39.270 --> 00:24:47.220
So now, because we have stored
the long-term memory facts

00:24:47.220 --> 00:24:50.490
in effect to database
as a hash vector,

00:24:50.490 --> 00:24:54.180
I can also use semantic search,
which my colleague was talking

00:24:54.180 --> 00:24:57.510
about before, to ask
questions about it.

00:24:57.510 --> 00:25:00.810
So if I'm going to say, "Hey,
what do you know about my work?"

00:25:00.810 --> 00:25:03.210
it's probably going to
give me a response back:

00:25:03.210 --> 00:25:04.230
"This is what I know.

00:25:04.230 --> 00:25:06.420
You like to work on
machine learning projects.

00:25:06.420 --> 00:25:08.430
Your main stack is this
and this and this."

00:25:08.430 --> 00:25:11.550
So it actually knows
what I'm all about.

00:25:11.550 --> 00:25:15.720
And if I can ask it, "Hey,
what activities do I enjoy?"

00:25:15.720 --> 00:25:17.730
it's going to give a
reason back as well.

00:25:17.730 --> 00:25:20.250
And you see on the left-hand
side, it doesn't have a clue.

00:25:20.250 --> 00:25:22.440
This AI system doesn't know.

00:25:22.440 --> 00:25:24.300
But on the right-hand
side, because we have

00:25:24.300 --> 00:25:28.800
that long-term memory in place,
it actually knows about me.

00:25:28.800 --> 00:25:33.030
What we have also done in this
specific scenario is we set

00:25:33.030 --> 00:25:34.920
TTLs, times to live,

00:25:34.920 --> 00:25:37.350
on the specific short-term
and long-term memory.

00:25:37.350 --> 00:25:40.230
So the short-term memory
is only kept for 30 days.

00:25:40.230 --> 00:25:42.030
And every single
conversation I have

00:25:42.030 --> 00:25:47.340
with this specific AI system is
going to be saved for 30 days,

00:25:47.340 --> 00:25:51.540
so I can jump to another
one, or I can jump to,

00:25:51.540 --> 00:25:54.300
"I also like sim racing,"
and stuff like that.

00:25:54.300 --> 00:25:56.550
Now, the long-term,
we're going to save

00:25:56.550 --> 00:26:00.060
that a little bit longer, over
a year, because I can be a user

00:26:00.060 --> 00:26:03.630
and I can go away for two
months, but the moment I'm going

00:26:03.630 --> 00:26:06.210
to come back and interacting
with the system,

00:26:06.210 --> 00:26:08.670
I want this system
to know about me.

00:26:08.670 --> 00:26:10.950
So basically, how
does this work?

00:26:10.950 --> 00:26:14.010
And I'm going to jump
to how it all works.

00:26:14.010 --> 00:26:17.400
So basically, you send a
message, "What are my hobbies?"

00:26:17.400 --> 00:26:19.860
This generates a
query embedding,

00:26:19.860 --> 00:26:23.220
because we're using the
text-embedding-3-small model

00:26:23.220 --> 00:26:25.140
from Azure OpenAI for this.

00:26:25.140 --> 00:26:27.480
It's going to do a vector
search around this.

00:26:27.480 --> 00:26:30.510
It's going to build some
context, and we are using AI

00:26:30.510 --> 00:26:32.640
to generate a response as well.

00:26:32.640 --> 00:26:35.550
Any new facts will
be saved as vectors,

00:26:35.550 --> 00:26:37.920
and we update the
conversational history.

00:26:37.920 --> 00:26:39.990
So we're all working
together basically

00:26:39.990 --> 00:26:44.940
to have a whole different
flow on this specific system.

00:26:44.940 --> 00:26:47.160
Now, if we go to the
long-term memory,

00:26:47.160 --> 00:26:48.750
this is actually
super interesting.

00:26:48.750 --> 00:26:51.990
So what we are saying
over here is, "Hey,

00:26:51.990 --> 00:26:55.560
we want to save any personal
information, any work,

00:26:55.560 --> 00:26:58.170
any preferences,
and any interest.

00:26:58.170 --> 00:27:01.800
But we don't want to save
every single thing."

00:27:01.800 --> 00:27:04.740
So we are going to give the AI
system or the different agents,

00:27:04.740 --> 00:27:07.260
we're going to give them
a set of instructions.

00:27:07.260 --> 00:27:10.890
And then every message that
I'm going to send, the agent

00:27:10.890 --> 00:27:13.500
and the AI system is
going to analyze it.

00:27:13.500 --> 00:27:16.530
Is this a permanent fact
or is it temporary?

00:27:16.530 --> 00:27:18.540
So if I'm going to say,
"I'm feeling tired today,"

00:27:18.540 --> 00:27:21.120
that's not going to be saved
as a long-term memory,

00:27:21.120 --> 00:27:22.560
because it's a temporary thing.

00:27:22.560 --> 00:27:24.900
I can feel fine tomorrow.

00:27:24.900 --> 00:27:26.130
Or, would this help me

00:27:26.130 --> 00:27:28.350
in personalizing
future conversation?

00:27:28.350 --> 00:27:31.680
Is the fact about the user
and not about the world?

00:27:31.680 --> 00:27:34.710
Then, we're using a special
tag if we want to save that,

00:27:34.710 --> 00:27:36.960
and we have the specific
decision criteria,

00:27:36.960 --> 00:27:40.290
and it's going to save it
or skip it specifically.

00:27:40.290 --> 00:27:43.470
So, in this specific scenario
that I also showcased, "Hey,

00:27:43.470 --> 00:27:45.180
I'm a Software
Developer at Microsoft,

00:27:45.180 --> 00:27:47.220
and I love playing
tennis on the weekends."

00:27:47.220 --> 00:27:48.810
It's a little bit different
than the one I showed,

00:27:48.810 --> 00:27:50.610
but it's roughly the same.

00:27:50.610 --> 00:27:54.390
We are basically identifying
multiple facts, and we're going

00:27:54.390 --> 00:27:56.580
to store those facts

00:27:56.580 --> 00:28:00.450
in the vector database while
using an embeddings model

00:28:00.450 --> 00:28:03.360
to create embeddings and then
store them all, obviously.

00:28:03.360 --> 00:28:04.860
So we're using vector hash

00:28:04.860 --> 00:28:06.900
for all these different
types of facts.

00:28:06.900 --> 00:28:09.390
So that's very,
very interesting.

00:28:09.390 --> 00:28:11.700
Now, let me jump to this one.

00:28:11.700 --> 00:28:13.020
How would this potentially look

00:28:13.020 --> 00:28:15.960
like from a AMR
storage perspective?

00:28:15.960 --> 00:28:18.720
So, as you can see over here,
we have the short-term memory.

00:28:18.720 --> 00:28:20.910
We have Phil talking
to the AI system.

00:28:20.910 --> 00:28:23.730
You have me and Thomas
talking to the AI system,

00:28:23.730 --> 00:28:25.800
and you see the TTLs
standing there as well.

00:28:25.800 --> 00:28:27.300
And I can actually
click this open,

00:28:27.300 --> 00:28:28.920
and this is basically
the whole conversation

00:28:28.920 --> 00:28:30.870
that we potentially had.

00:28:30.870 --> 00:28:33.060
But then if I go to
the vector facts,

00:28:33.060 --> 00:28:36.150
you see these are all the facts
that we have stored: interest,

00:28:36.150 --> 00:28:39.240
personal, work, and
we tag them as well.

00:28:39.240 --> 00:28:43.740
So this allows you to give
multiple agents access

00:28:43.740 --> 00:28:47.940
to durable information or to
the short-term information

00:28:47.940 --> 00:28:49.860
rapidly quick.

00:28:49.860 --> 00:28:50.670
And now think about it,

00:28:50.670 --> 00:28:53.940
if you have multiple agents
working together, they all have

00:28:53.940 --> 00:28:57.810
that same information, and they
can access that rapidly quick,

00:28:57.810 --> 00:29:00.300
increasing the performance
of your whole workflow,

00:29:00.300 --> 00:29:01.950
and therefore giving
your customers a way

00:29:01.950 --> 00:29:03.570
better experience.

00:29:03.570 --> 00:29:08.580
If you think about a travel
companion type of application,

00:29:08.580 --> 00:29:12.840
this travel companion
knows that I like Airbnbs

00:29:12.840 --> 00:29:14.850
and early flights, and
I don't need to specify

00:29:14.850 --> 00:29:17.970
that every single time
I'm interacting with it.

00:29:17.970 --> 00:29:20.700
So every single time I'm
talking to the system,

00:29:20.700 --> 00:29:23.970
and it's helping me to book my
travel, it already knows this,

00:29:23.970 --> 00:29:27.810
and it gives me a way better
experience, and I get as a user

00:29:27.810 --> 00:29:31.830
to a faster result that
I'm looking for --

00:29:31.830 --> 00:29:35.340
booking my flight without
telling it every single thing.

00:29:35.340 --> 00:29:38.700
The agent memory capability
of AMR also fully integrates

00:29:38.700 --> 00:29:42.540
with the agent framework, the
Microsoft Agent Framework,

00:29:42.540 --> 00:29:45.900
so that's also something
that is natively embedded

00:29:45.900 --> 00:29:48.390
and supported around
that as well.

00:29:48.390 --> 00:29:50.970
So that was my quick
demo around this

00:29:50.970 --> 00:29:53.760
and why I think agent
memory and using AMR

00:29:53.760 --> 00:29:57.120
for agent memory is a
very interesting way

00:29:57.120 --> 00:30:00.360
of giving your agents
that specific information

00:30:00.360 --> 00:30:04.980
that they need to have those
high-fulfilling AI tasks.

00:30:04.980 --> 00:30:07.800
So thanks, everyone, for
listening to us and learning

00:30:07.800 --> 00:30:12.720
about semantic caching,
semantic search, agent memory,

00:30:12.720 --> 00:30:16.470
and Azure Managed Redis as the
foundation for all of that.

00:30:16.470 --> 00:30:18.570
I want to say a big
thanks to Phil and Shruti

00:30:18.570 --> 00:30:20.220
for doing this together with me.

